INT. COMMUNITY CENTRE - POTTERY CLASS - EVENING

Four wheels spin. Slip splashes the floor. A kiln sighs in the corner.

YETUNDE ADEBAYO, 50s, instructor, stands at the front with a clay bowl held aloft. EPHRAIM PRUITT, 70, retired, hunches over a collapsing vase. MARISKA JANSEN, 30s, dyed blue hair, flicks clay off her fingers. WILFRED TANG, 40s, accountant, wears a plastic apron over a three-piece suit.

YETUNDE
(to the class)
Centre your clay, everyone. Centre!

MARISKA
(to Ephraim)
Yours looks like a hat.

EPHRAIM
It's meant to.

WILFRED
(muttering, to himself)
Not my best vase. Not my best vase at all.

YETUNDE
Wilfred, pressure with the heel of your hand.

WILFRED
That's what I'm doing.

His vase leans, folds, and slides off the wheel.

EPHRAIM
(to Wilfred)
Better luck.

YETUNDE
(to the room)
Take a breath. Pottery is patience.

The kiln timer BUZZES. Everyone jumps. Mariska flings a handful of slip across Ephraim's shirt.

MARISKA
Sorry!

EPHRAIM
(to himself)
Should've taken up golf.

Yetunde sets down her bowl. Sighs. Looks at the wreckage. Wilfred looks at the vase on the floor.

WILFRED
(soft)
Would anyone mind if I just threw it out?
